{
  "segments": [
    {
      "id": 100,
      "text": "A browser mean you are completely trusting a third party company with your keys, which is incredibly risky for large amounts."
    },
    {
      "id": 101,
      "text": "This raises an important question about security, which is why cybersecurity professionals strongly advocate for"
    },
    {
      "id": 102,
      "text": "hardware signing devices. OK, hardware devices. Those are the ones that look a bit like a USB thumb drive, right?"
    },
    {
      "id": 103,
      "text": "Yep. Specialized offline hardware. Why are they so much safer? Because they generate and store your private cryptographic keys completely"
    },
    {
      "id": 104,
      "text": "offline. So they never touch the Internet. Exactly. When you want to send a transaction, you plug the device into your computer."
    },
    {
      "id": 105,
      "text": "The computer sends the unsigned transaction to the hardware device. The physical chip inside the device"
    },
    {
      "id": 106,
      "text": "signs the transaction cryptographically and then sends only the signed approval back to the computer."
    },
    {
      "id": 107,
      "text": "So your private key never actually touches your Internet"
    },
    {
      "id": 108,
      "text": "connected computer. Never. Even if your laptop is completely riddled with viruses, the hackers cannot steal the. That's"
    },
    {
      "id": 109,
      "text": "brilliant. Now, when these wallets talk to the network, there's a big distinction in the source material between running a full node"
    },
    {
      "id": 110,
      "text": "and a lightweight client. Yes. Huge difference. A full node downloads and validates the entire history of Bitcoin. Every"
    },
    {
      "id": 111,
      "text": "transaction by every user ever, which requires a massive amount of hard drive space and computing"
    },
    {
      "id": 112,
      "text": "resources. Right. But lightweight clients, sometimes called Simplified Payment Verification or SPV clients,"
    },
    {
      "id": 113,
      "text": "are what most people have on their phones, right? Exactly. They rely on those full nodes to do the heavy lifting of storing the entire blockchain."
    },
    {
      "id": 114,
      "text": "Okay. But they use cryptographic proofs to verify their own specific transactions without having to trust the full node"
    },
    {
      "id": 115,
      "text": "blindly. I look at it like this: Running a full node is like doing your own accounting by auditing every single grocery"
    },
    {
      "id": 116,
      "text": "receipt, every paycheck, every penny spent in the entire global economy since the dawn of time, just to be absolutely"
    },
    {
      "id": 117,
      "text": "sure your own balance is correct. That is an intense way to do accounting. Right. Whereas using a lightweight client is"
    },
    {
      "id": 118,
      "text": "like calling the bank to check your balance, but you still keep the physical cash in your own pocket. That's a great analogy. And that"
    },
    {
      "id": 119,
      "text": "brings up the most critical concept in all of cryptocurrency: Who actually controls the money? Right."
    }
  ]
}
